    "d_sv_content": {
      "id": "29761",
      "name": "Using nanoparticles to deliver anti-tumor genes to pet dogs with high-grade gliomas",
      "tagline": "Utilizing microRNA encapsulated in nanoparticles in the treatment of high-grade glioma",
      "speciesListDisplay": "",
      "ageRange": "",
      "studyType": "",
      "description": "<p>Gliomas are among the most common and most aggressive primary brain tumors in pet dogs. Established definitive treatment options include surgery, radiation therapy, or a combination of these treatments. Surgical resection alone does not improve survival times achieved with supportive care alone, and is therefore not recommended. Radiation therapy provides an average of 14 months of survival time. The purpose of this study is to investigate a novel treatment option with the goal being to improve outcomes for dogs with high grade gliomas. Treatment in this study consists of injections of nanoparticles (NPs) that target brain tumor cells and result in their death. </p><p><br></p><p>This is a single site, prospective, three arm clinical trial with no control (placebo) group. Each patient will be randomly assigned into one of these three groups. The treatment arms are:</p><p><br></p><p>1) surgical resection + nanoparticle administration</p><p>2) surgical resection + stereotactic radiation therapy</p><p>3) surgical resection + stereotactic radiation therapy + nanoparticle administration</p>",
      "requirements": "",
      "responsibilities": "",
      "whoCan": "",
      "benefits": "",
      "compensation": "",
      "videoUrl": "",
      "videoThumbUrl": "",
      "timeCommitment": "",
