    "d_sv_content": {
      "id": "15215",
      "name": "Clinical trial of a targeted chemotherapy drug delivered by a catheter to treat dogs with glioma brain tumors",
      "tagline": "New therapy for canine glioma brain tumors",
      "speciesListDisplay": "",
      "ageRange": "",
      "studyType": "",
      "description": "<p>Dogs and humans are the only species in which primary brain tumors are common. In dogs, a type of malignant tumor called a glioma accounts for approximately 35% of all spontaneous primary brain tumors. Even with treatment, the prognosis for both dogs and humans with gliomas is poor, and there is no cure. Current therapies such as radiation aim at extending quantity and improving quality of life. In this clinical trial, we are testing the safety and effectiveness of molecularly targeted cytotoxins, which are chemotherapeutic drugs, delivered directly to the patient's brain tumor. Developed by researchers at the Thomas K. Hearn Brain Tumor Research Centerat the Wake Forest School of Medicine, the unique chemotherapeutic drugs used in this trial are designed to affect only cancerous cells, and not normal brain tissue. Earlier generations of these molecularly targeted cyotoxins have been used safely and effectively in dogs with gliomas. Using specialized catheters the cytotoxins are infused directly into the tumor over a several hour period. The drug treatment is monitored continuously with MRI to allow the neurosurgeon to precisely track the drug delivery. </p><p>\n</p><p>**As a dog owner, how can I participate in this trial? **\nIf you suspect that your dog may have a brain tumor, you should arrange for an appointment with your local veterinarian. They will perform a complete neurological examination, which is the first and fundamental step in establishing a diagnosis in animals with brain disease. It is also a prerequisite for them to determine which additional diagnostic tests to recommend. However, advanced imaging studies of the brain, such as MRI, interpreted in conjunction with the neurological examination and other supporting tests, are usually necessary to best characterize diseases of the brain. If your local veterinarian cannot perform an MRI, they will often refer you to a veterinary neurologist who offers this service. Our Neurology and Neurosurgery Service works on a referral only basis, which means that your local veterinarian must first evaluate your pet and arrange for an appointment to see one of our specialists. In order to be eligible for this specific clinical trial, your dog must have an MRI of the brain that demonstrates an abnormality consistent with a glioma. </p><p>\n</p><p>Clinical Trial Inclusion Criteria: \n- Dogs of any age, breed, or sex between 3-45 kg in body weight\n- Clinical signs of mild to moderate neurologic dysfunction referable to the brain\n- MRI evidence of a single telencephalic intra-axial mass lesion consistent with a glioma\n- No clinical or other diagnostic evidence of other significant systemic disease. \n- Dogs must have gross, measurable disease (&gt; 1cm in diameter) on a current MRI </p><p>\n</p><p>We are happy to evaluate clinical and imaging findings to determine patient eligibility for this clinical trial. </p><p>\n</p><p>To expedite this process, please complete and return our Neurology Clinical Trial Consultation Form: https://virginiatech.qualtrics.com/SE/?SID=SV_2ct6Cm6EegrTga1.</p>",
      "requirements": "",
      "responsibilities": "",
      "whoCan": "",
      "benefits": "",
      "compensation": "Diagnosis must be confirmed by obtaining an MRI scan at owner's expense. The costs of the CED treatment procedure and follow-up MRI scans for individual dogs will be covered by the trial.",
      "videoUrl": "",
      "videoThumbUrl": "",
      "timeCommitment": "",
      "healthyVolunteer": "",
      "targetGender": "",
      "recruitmentTarget": null
    },
