    "d_sv_content": {
      "id": "29760",
      "name": "Combining Zika virus vaccines and autologous tumor lysates in pet dogs with high-grade gliomas",
      "tagline": "Stimulating the immune system in the treatment of high-grade glioma",
      "speciesListDisplay": "",
      "ageRange": "",
      "studyType": "",
      "description": "<p>Gliomas are among the most common and most aggressive primary brain tumors in pet dogs. Established definitive treatment options include surgery, radiation therapy, or a combination of these treatments. Surgical resection alone does not improve survival times achieved with supportive care alone, and is therefore not recommended. Radiation therapy provides an average of 14 months of survival time. The purpose of this study is to investigate a novel treatment option with the goal being to improve outcomes for dogs with high grade gliomas. Treatment in this study consists of removal of the brain tumor, followed by injections of a Zika virus vaccine along with anti-tumor vaccines. The combination is meant to trigger an immune response against any tumor cells that remain in the brain after surgery. These injections are done under the skin.</p><p><br></p><p>This is a single site, prospective, single arm clinical trial with no control (placebo) group. Treatment will consist of surgical removal of the tumor, then three weekly injections of the virus along with anti-tumor vaccines. Dogs will undergo recheck MRIs every four months for a year or until there is evidence of tumor regrowth, whichever comes first.</p>",
